## 1. Introduction
Nowadays, energy crises and serious environmental problems are the major challenges faced by human beings [1,2]. Therefore, researchers have paid more attention to sustainable energy conversion and clean energy [3,4]. Photocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to valuable fuels is considered as an effective way to address the energy issues and mitigate the greenhouse effect [5–10]. However, the efficiency of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoreduction is relatively low due to the unsatisfied activity of photocatalysts and inertia of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules [11,12]. To overcome these drawbacks, enormous efforts have been made by scientists. Lots of semiconductor materials were prepared and investigated in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction, such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , a wellknown photocatalyst that has been extensively studied [13–20]. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based composites were also prepared to enhance visible-light absorption and charge separation efficiency [21–23].
Recently, titanium-based metal organic frameworks (MOFs), such as MIL-125 (Ti), have been studied as photocatalysts due to their enhanced surface areas and high intrinsic microporosity [24–26]. Nevertheless, the photocatalytic performance of MIL125(Ti) is not comparable to that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which is mainly limited by titanium-based MOFs’ low charge exciton efficiency, fast charge recombination, and poor stability [27,28]. Meanwhile, MIL-125(Ti) can be excited only by UV-light [29,30]. To boost the photocatalytic activity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction, an ideal strategy is to functionally integrate the Titanium-based MOFs with an inorganic semiconductor [31–33]. The well-organized MIL-125(Ti) can prevent the aggregation of semiconductor nanoparticles and improve the transfer and separation efficiency of photogenerated charges through the synergy between semiconductors [34,35]. Some methods can be adopted to enhance visible-light absorption [36,37]. One is to modify MIL-125(Ti) through ligand functionalization of terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ [38,39]. For example, after aminomodification of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC, the obtained $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) shows obvious visible light absorption compared to the original MIL-125(Ti) [40]. Another method is to decorate MIL-125(Ti) or MIL-125(Ti)-based hybrids with photosensitizers such as noble metals and narrow band gap metal chalcogenides [41]. For example, ternary $\mathrm{CdS/Pt/MIL}{\cdot}125\mathrm{(Ti)}$ heterostructure hybrid was synthesized by $\gamma$ -ray irradiation and exhibited excellent visible-light photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production [42]. In this hybrid catalyst, the porous MIL-125(Ti) matrix restrained the aggregation of Pt and CdS nanoparticles. Meanwhile, the formed ternary heterostructure efficiently suppressed the photogenerated carrier recombination. Based on the above discussions, Ti-based MOFs have been widely utilized as sacrificial precursors to prepare hybrid photocatalysts featuring controllable compositions and complex structures. However, the controllable synthesis of Ti-based MOF-derived photocatalysts with desirable compositions, architectures, and light absorption properties still remains a great challenge.
In this work, we synthesized ZnSe sensitized hierarchical MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ hollow nanodisks (HNDs) through a two-step solvothermal and subsequent selenylation process (Scheme 1). The solvothermal reaction is a time-dependent composition control experiment that can realize the transformation of MIL-125 (Ti) to $Z\mathrm{n}$ -doped MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The following selenylation process leads to the formation of ternary hybrids of ZnSe sensitized hierarchical $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow nanodisks (MIL125(Ti $\mathsf{)}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs). In ternary hybrid, MI $\scriptstyle[\mathbf{L}-125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ presents hierarchical hollow sandwich-like heterostructure nanodisks in which $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets are distributed on both sides of MIL-125(Ti) nanoshell. The in situ formation of ZnSe nanoparticles makes the MIL-125(Ti) $\mathsf{\Pi}_{|\mathbb{Q}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}}$ HNDs hybrid show enhanced visible-light absorption. Thus, the optimized hybrid possesses the merits of abundant catalytic sites, efficient charge separation, and enhanced visible-light absorption, resulting in the enhanced photocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction performance and recyclability compared with single-component catalysts (MIL-125(Ti), $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ) and binary MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hybrid catalyst.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and reagents
Tetrabutyl titanate $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{16}H_{36}O_{4}T i}$ , $99.0\%$ , Tianjin Komiou Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd)), terephthalic acid $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}B D C}$ , $99.0\%$ , Tianjin Komiou Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd), $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $99.0\%$ , Tianjin Damao Chemical Reagent Factory), Sodium borohydride $(N a B H_{4},$ $99.0\%$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd), N, $N\mathrm{.}$ - Dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ , Aladdin Industrial Co., Ltd), methanol $\mathrm{\cdot}C H_{3}O H$ , $99.5\%$ , Tianjin Fuyu Fine Chemical Co., Ltd), ethanol $\mathrm{CH}_{3}C\mathrm{H}_{2}O\mathrm{H}$ , $99.5\%$ , Tianjin Fuyu Fine Chemical Co., Ltd), Selenium powder (Se, $99.95\%$ , Xingta Chemical Plant). All chemical reagents are analytically pure and used without purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of MIL-125(Ti)
The MIL-125(Ti) nanodisks with good dispersion and uniformity were prepared according to a modified method reported previously [43]. Typically, $1.5\mathrm{~g~}$ of terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ was dissolved in $36~\mathrm{mL}$ of a mixed solution of methanol (MeOH) and N, $N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) (the volume ratio of MeOH: DMF is 1:3) under stirring. Then $0.8~\mathrm{mL}$ of tetrabutyl titanate (TBOT) was added dropwise to the above mixed solution. After stirring for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the mixture was transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon autoclave. After the autoclave was heated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the MIL-125 (Ti) product was obtained and washed with ethanol solution, and then dried at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
2.3. Synthesis of Zn-MIL-125(Ti)@TiO2 HNDs:
The prepared MIL-125(Ti) $\mathrm{30~mg}$ was added to $12~\mathrm{mL}$ of a mixed solution of methanol (MeOH) and N, $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) (the volume ratio of MeOH:DMF is 1:3) under stirring. Then a amount of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{30mg)}$ was add to the mixed solution. After the mixture was stirred for 30 min and transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon autoclave. After the autoclave was heated at $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ the obtained product was washed with ethanol solution, and the product was dried at $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
The synthesis process of MI $\mathrm{L}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ is similar to that of $Z_{\mathrm{n-MIL-}1}25(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The only difference is that no $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})}}$ · $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was introduced.
The synthesis process of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is similar to that of MIL$125\mathrm{Ti@TiO_{2}}$ . The only difference is that the solvothermal time was extended to $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
2.4. Synthesis of MIL-125(Ti)@TiO2\ZnSe HNDs:
$8.8\mathrm{mg}$ of Se powder and $17.8\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ were added to $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol solution under stirring. Then, $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of Zn-MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ HNDs were added into the mixture above. The resulting mixture was transferred to the Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{\mathrm{~1~h~}}$ . The obtained material was rinsed with absolute ethanol and then dried at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight.
The synthesis process of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZnSe}$ is similar to that of MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs, but using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to replace MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .
## 2.5. Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained from a diffractometer (SmartLab, 9 KW, CuKa radiation). The morphology and structures were observed on Sigma 500 scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and JEM-F200 transmission electron microscopy (TEM). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was implemented on AXIS-ULTRA DLD-600 W instrument using a Al Ka X-ray source. The surface area and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption curves were determined on an ASAP 2420 instrument (Micromeritics). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were tested from Nicolet IS-50 Spectrometer. UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) were acquired on a Lambda 950
UV–vis spectrophotometer (Perkin-Elmer). The photoluminescence spectra (PL) were acquired on a FLS920P Edinburgh Instrument.
## 2.6. Photoelectrochemical measurements
Photocurrent response and Mott–Schottky measurements were carried out on a BAS100B electrochemical work station $(0.5\mathrm{~M~Na}_{2}.$ - $S0_{4}\mathrm{\cdot}$ . Electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were acquired from an electrochemical workstation (Zahner Elektrik) under dark condition. Saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and platinum wire were used as reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. The light source was a 300 W Xe lamp.
## 2.7. Photocatalytic tests for $C O_{2}$ reduction
The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction tests were conducted in a gas–solid reactor $\mathrm{\Delta}80\ \mathrm{mL}$ , Perfectlight, China). A 300 W Xe lamp was used as light source $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}},$ ). $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of photocatalyst powder was first dispersed on a glass sheet in the reactor. The distance from the class sheet to light source is $8\mathrm{cm}$ , and the geometric area of catalyst powder is $6.25~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Then, high-purity ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was passed through the reactor via deionized water for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ to eliminate air under ambient pressure. After that, the reactor was sealed and irradiated. A circulation water system was applied to keep the reactor temperature at about $20\ {^{\circ}C}.$ . 1 mL gas was taken and quantified with GC7900 gas chromatograph every $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ reaction.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterizations of catalysts
The phase structures of the samples were confirmed by X-ray diffraction (XRD) characterization. As shown in Fig. 1a, the diffraction peaks at 2h at $6.9^{\circ}$ , $9.8^{\circ}$ , $11.8^{\circ}$ , and $19.7^{\circ}$ prove the successful preparation of MIL-125(Ti) precursor [44]. Likewise, the diffraction peaks of MIL-125(Ti) exist in the XRD patterns of MIL- $.125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ HNDs and MIL- $125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs, but the diffraction peak intensity ratio of MIL- $.125(\mathrm{Ti})/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ gradually decreased. It indicates the gradual conversion of MIL-125(Ti) to anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the increase of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ crystallinity. Moreover, the timedependent solvothermal reactions also confirm that MIL-125(Ti) could be successively converted to M $\mathsf{I I L}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with extending solvothermal reaction time from $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ to $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ , which can be proved by the XRD patterns (Fig. S1-5). When the solvothermal time is enough long $\left(\geq4\mathrm{{h}}\right)$ , MIL-125(Ti) was completely changed to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and only $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoplates composed of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets were obtained (Fig. S4, 5). The diffraction peaks ascribed to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZnSe can be clearly observed in the XRD pattern of MIL-125 $\mathrm{(Ti)}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs (Fig. 1a). The FTIR spectra were also listed in Fig. 1b to analyze the structure and component of catalysts. In these FTIR spectra, the strong and wide absorption band at approximately $3443\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the stretching vibration of the $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ groups of the adsorbed water [45]. In the FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the band at about $651~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ may be attributed to the stretching modes of Ti-O in $\mathrm{TiO}_{6}$ unit [46]. In the FTIR spectrum of MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnS}$ e HNDs, apart from the band ascribed to Ti-O in $\mathrm{TiO}_{6}$ unit of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the absorption bands at around 1478 and $1559\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the $\mathsf{v}(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{C})$ skeletal vibration of benzene ring contained in MIL-125(Ti), and the other absorption bands at 796 and $1293~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were ascribed to the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ out-of-plane and in-plane bending vibrations, respectively [47]. The results further confirm the existence of MIL-125(Ti) in the hybrid.
The morphologies and structure of the samples were also characterized. As shown in SEM image (Fig. 2a), the obtained MIL-125 (Ti) precursor possessed a uniform disk-like morphology with smooth surface. The average particle size was about $2\pm0.1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The solid nature of the MIL-125(Ti) nanodisks was revealed by the TEM image (inset of Fig. 2a). For the MIL- $.125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ samples with different contents of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Fig. S1-5) and MIL-125(Ti) $\mathtt{@T i O_{2}}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs (Fig. 2b), the disk-like morphology was kept, but the surface was covered with nanosheets (Fig. 2c). Upon close observation, one can see the broken MI $-125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs are hollow space inside. The nanodisk shell is a sandwiched-like structure, and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets are distributed on both sides of
MIL-125(Ti) layer (Fig. S6). Low-magnified TEM image further confirms the formation of hollow structure (Fig. 2d). High-magnified TEM image indicates that the shell is porous and covered by nanosheets (Fig. 2e). In the High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Fig. 2f), the lattice spacings of 0.328 and $0.352\ \mathrm{nm}$ correspond to the (111) plane of ZnSe and the (101) crystal plane of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively [48]. Scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) image (Fig. 2g) and element mappings (Fig. 2h-l) of MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}^{\cdot}$ \ZnSe HNDs reveal the uniform distribution of C, O, Ti, Zn, and Se in the entire hollow nanodisk. These results demonstrate that the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ anchored MIL-125(Ti) nanodisks are successfully converted to ZnSe sensitized hierarchical MI $\mathsf{L}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow nanodisks $(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}\backslash\mathrm{Zn}S\mathrm{e}$ HNDs) through the sequent hydrothermal and selenylation reactions.
The element chemical states of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}{\backslash}\mathrm{Zn}S\mathrm{e}$ HNDs sample were elucidated by XPS technique. The survey XPS spectrum (Fig. 3a) shows that the MIL-125(Ti) $\mathsf{\Pi}_{|\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}}$ HNDs sample mainly consists of Ti, Zn, Se, O, and C elements. The high-resolution
XPS Ti 2p, O 1 s, Zn 2p, Se 3d, and C 1 s spestra of MIL-125(Ti) $\mathtt{@T i O_{2}}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs were also provided. Fig. 3b shows highresolution Ti $2{\tt p}$ spectrum. The binding energy values of Ti $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ti $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ are 458.1 and $463.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively, indicating that titanium bounded to oxygen remains in $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ for the titanium-oxo cluster [49]. The O 1 s spectrum in Fig. 3c can be fitted into three peaks. The peak at $533.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to the OH group [50]. The peaks at 531.6 and $530.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to $c{-}0$ and titanium-oxo cluster, respectively [51,52]. The peaks centered at 1022.2 and 1045.5 eV (Fig. 3d) correspond to Zn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively [53]. As for Se 3d XPS spectrum (Fig. 3e), the peaks at 61.2 and $62.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be indexed to Se $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Se $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , respectively [54]. The C 1 s XPS spectrum (Fig. 3f) shows three peaks at 284.9, 286.2, and $288.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C},$ $c{-}0$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , respectively [55,56].
Nitrogen adsorption desorption isotherms and the corresponding results are listed in Fig. 4a and Table S1 (SI). MIL-125 (Ti) shows typical type I isotherm, indicating the microporous structure with a large surface area $(884\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})\mathrm{~.~}$ . Pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mainly possesses mesoporous structure and relatively low surface area $(214~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Obviously, MIL- $.125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ HNDs and MIL-125 $\mathrm{(Ti)}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs possess bimodel pore-size (microporous and mesoporous) distribution (Fig. 4b), and their surface area values (Table S1) are similar and located between those of MIL-125 (Ti) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , demonstrating the formation of heterostructure hybrids with a bimodal pore-size distribution, which is favorable for mass transfer.
To investigate the optical absorption property of these samples, UV vis diffuse reflectance spectra were examined. UV vis absorption spectra of different samples are shown in Fig. 4c. Both MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ show obvious UV-light absorption due to their wide band gaps. Compared with MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ZnSe showed an enhanced visible-light absorption in the range from 400 to $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ due to its proper band gap (Fig. S13). Moreover, the MIL- $.125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs sample shows an enhanced visible-light absorption in the region from 400 to $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ owing to the sensitization of narrow-bandgap ZnSe nanoparticles. In Fig. 4d, the corresponding band gap energies (calculated from plots of Kubelka Munk functions vs. light energy) of MIL-125(Ti), MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}\backslash\mathrm{Zn}S\mathrm{e}$ HNDs gradually decrease due to the introduction of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZnSe.
## 3.2. Photocatalytic activity tests
The photocatalytic activities of different catalysts were evaluated by photoreduction ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 5a-c, in this photocatalytic system, CO is the main product and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is the byproduct. It is found that both MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibited a relatively low photocatalytic CO production performance due to the rapid charge recombination and only UV-light absorption. However, the yield rates of CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ over the binary MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ catalyst are significantly higher than those over the singlecomponent catalysts (MIL-125(Ti), $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ) and physical mixture of MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Fig. S8), which is due to the efficient charge separation and bimodal pore-size distribution in binary MIL-125 $\mathrm{(Ti)}\ @\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, the MI $\_{125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}}$ with the moderate ratio of MIL $-125(\mathrm{Ti})/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ showed the optimized photocatalytic performance (Fig. S7). Excess $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ on the outer surface of MIL125(Ti) can decrease surface area and active sites of MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . As expected, compared with single-component and binary hybrid catalysts $(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe})$ , the MIL-125(Ti) $\mathtt{@T i O_{2}}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs ternary hybrid catalyst exhibited the highest photocatalytic performance (Fig. 5, Fig. S9), and the yield rates of CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ reached 513.1 and $45.1\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. Moreover, the energy conversion efficiency and apparent quantum efficiency of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ excitation wavelength are about $0.31\%$ and $2.60\%$ , respectively, which are much superior to those of MIL-125(Ti), $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Table S3). Moreover, the MIL-125(Ti) $\mathsf{\Pi}_{|\mathrm{(0TiO_{2})}|\mathrm{ZnSe}}$ HNDs catalyst exhibits excellent durability, which is one of the critical indexes of photocatalyst. Fig. 5d shows that the MIL-125(Ti) $\mathtt{@T i O_{2}}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs photocatalyst exhibits a considerable durability during the cycle tests. The yield rates of CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ show no obvious decrease, and the crystal structure and morphology of MIL-125 $\mathrm{\Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs also have no obvious change after cycling tests. The results indicate the favorable stability of MIL-125(Ti) $\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs in the photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction tests. In contrast, pure MIL-125(Ti) shows some decrease in activity (Fig. S10). It proves that the formation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets outer layer can well protect the MIL-125(Ti) inner core layer, thus enhancing its stability. In addition, the photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction tests under visible light irradiation were also carried out. As shown in Fig. S12, both
MIL-125(Ti) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibit only very low visible light activity due to their wide band gaps. For comparison, the MIL-125(Ti) $\textcircled{\sc{\omega}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs sample showed a significantly enhanced visible light photocatalytic activity owing to the sensitization of narrow bandgap ZnSe nanoparticles. But its photocatalytic activity is still lower than that under stimulated solar light (AM1.5) irradiation due to the limited light absorption range.
The enhanced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction performance of MIL-125(Ti) $\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs can be ascribed to several advantages. First, the constructed ternary hollow heterostructure can greatly promote the separation of photogenerated charge, which was proved by the following PL and electrochemical tests (Fig. 7). In MIL-125 $\mathrm{(Ti)}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs, besides the direct photogenerated charge transfer between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZnSe, the photogenerated electrons in the conduction band (CB) of ZnSe can also transfer to the CB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ via MIL-125(Ti) matrix because some of ZnSe nanoparticles are in situ formed in the MIL-125(Ti) nanoshell. Both charge transfer routes greatly promote the charge carrier separation, thus boosting ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction performance. Second, the porous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell composed of ultrathin $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets and bimodal pore-size distribution can expose abundant unsaturated titanium-oxo cluster to adsorb and activate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules and enhance photoredox catalysis by promoting mass/charge transfer [57]. Third, the visible light absorption property of hybrid is greatly promoted by ZnSe sensitization (Fig. 4c). Additionally, the internal void space of MI $-125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs can enhance incident light scattering and utilization [58].
Some control experiments were also carried out. No CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ were detected under dark condition or without catalyst (Fig. 6a), further proving that the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction was driven by photocatalyst under simulated solar light irradiation. Similarly, when using argon gas to replace ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , no CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ were detected while keeping the other conditions unchanged. Moreover, the yields of CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ evolution over the crushed MIL-125(Ti) $\mathsf{\Pi}_{|\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}}$ HNDs are lower than that of M $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{IL}}-125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs, demonstrating the superiority of hierarchical hollow nanodisk heterostructure. Meanwhile, the $0_{2}$ evolution rate over $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs was also measured to be $346.7~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , which is much higher than that over the other photocatalysts (Fig. S11), and the electrons generated from water oxidation slightly outnumber the electrons participating in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. The isotopically labeled experiments show that the $^{13}{\mathsf{C O}}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ signals at $m/z=29$ and $m/z=17$ appeared in the mass spectra (Fig. 6b) after the photocatalytic reaction. The results proved that the produced $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and CO are indeed originating from $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ gas in the photocatalytic reduction process.
For photocatalysts, their ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability plays an important role in the photoreduction reaction. The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms over these samples show that MIL-125(Ti) $\mathsf{\Pi}_{|\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}}$ HNDs has a higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capability than the pristine MIL-125(Ti) (Fig. 6c). Despites the relatively low surface area, the increased ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capability of MIL-125(Ti) $\mathtt{@T i O_{2}}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs is within expectation because the formation of hollow nanodisk-like heterostructured hybrid and surfaceanchored cocatalyst nanoparticles can enhance the interactions with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules. The enhanced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability of the MIL-125(Ti) $\mathsf{\Pi}_{|\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}}$ HNDs ternary hybrid was investigated through ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ temperature programmed desorption (TPD). All these samples existed one peak in their ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TPD}}$ curves (Fig. 6d). By comparison, $\Lambda\mathrm{IL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs sample possesses the highest ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ desorption peak temperature $(\sim254~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ with the largest peak area, while the pristine MIL-125(Ti) possesses the lowest ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ desorption peak temperature $(\sim75~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ because of an acid surface. This indicates that the formation of MIL-125(Ti) $\mathtt{@T i O_{2}}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs can enhance surface basicity and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption ability.
In order to further reveal the transfer/separation of photogenerated carriers in MIL-125(Ti) $\textcircled{\sc{\omega}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs and the influence on the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction activity, photoluminescence (PL) spectra are tested. The steady-state PL spectra from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and MIL-125(Ti) show two strong emission peaks at 410 and $430~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Fig. 7a). The hybrids samples provide a similar PL spectrum to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and MIL-125(Ti), but their PL intensities show obvious decreased. Especially the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs sample shows significant fluorescence quenching, which is attributed to the more effective charge separation because of the presence of ternary heterojunctions. The charge transfer dynamics were also studied by time-resolved photoluminescence spectroscopy (TRPL) (Fig. 7b). Obviously, the MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}^{\prime}$ \ZnSe HNDs sample provides a longer average fluorescence lifetime than MIL-125(Ti), $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and MI $\_{125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}}$ (Table S4). It demonstrates that the formation of ternary heterojunctions could significantly inhibit charge recombination in MIL-125(Ti) $\varpi\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ \ZnSe HNDs. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and photocurrent measurements further support the above results. As shown in Fig. 7c, the MIL-125(Ti) $\mathsf{\Pi}_{|\mathrm{(0TiO_{2})}|\mathrm{ZnSe}}$ HNDs sample exhibits the smallest semicircle at high frequencies, suggesting the smallest interfacial electron transfer resistance. Meanwhile, in the transient photocurrent plots (Fig. 7d), the MIL $-125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs sample exhibits the highest photocurrent response. The results suggest that the formation of ternary heterojunctions promotes the separation of photogenerated carriers.
Based on the above results, a feasible mechanism for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs is provided in Scheme 2. The energy level configuration of MIL-125(Ti) $\textcircled{\sc{\omega}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}|\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs was determined by combining with band gaps (Fig. 4d, Fig. S13), valence band XPS spectra and Mott-Schottky plots (Fig. S14). The energy band parameters are listed in Table S5. The proper energy level configuration and the close contact with each other lead to the formation of ternary heterojunctions. Thus, in $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}{\backslash}\mathrm{Zn}S e$ HNDs, direct photogenerated charge transfer can happen between the MIL-125 (Ti), $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ZnSe. Meanwhile, MIL-125(Ti) as the hollow nanodisk matrix can connect ZnSe nanoparticles and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets, thus the photogenerated electrons in the conduction band (CB) of
ZnSe can also be transferred to that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets via MIL125(Ti) matrix due to their interfacial interaction and proper energy positions. Thus, the multi-channel charge transfer routes generate more electrons to participate in the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction reaction to produce CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Meanwhile, holes in the valence band (VB) of ZnSe oxidize the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules to generate oxygen gas, which strongly inhibit the photocorrosion of ZnSe nanoparticles, hence improving the stability of MIL-125(Ti) $\textcircled{\underline{{\omega}}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\backslash\mathrm{ZnSe}$ HNDs photocatalyst. The multi-channel charge transfer routes in ternary heterostructure hollow nanodisks contribute to the charge transfer/separation and extend the lifespan of charge-separated state, thus boosting photoreduction $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ performance.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we demonstrated a two-step solvothermal and selenylation reaction route to prepare ZnSe sensitized hierarchical M $\bar{1}\mathrm{I}\mathrm{L}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ hollow sandwich-like heterostructure nanodisks, which possess several unique features that are beneficial to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction. Efficient charge separation was realized via ternary heterojunctions with matched energy band gap and multi-channel charge transfer routes. Meanwhile, hollow sandwich-like nanodisk architecture with bimodal pore-size distribution provided more active sites and improved incident light utilization. Moreover, ZnSe sensitization resulted in high visible light absorption. All these positive merits can benefit the photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reaction. As expected, ZnSe sensitized hierarchical MIL- $-125(\mathrm{Ti})@\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}$ hollow nanodisks exhibited significantly higher yields of CO $(513.1\ \mathrm{\textmumol\g}^{-1}.\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $:45.1\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1})$ than those of the control samples and most of the recent reports [59,60]. Additionally, the recyclability of the hybrid photocatalyst was remarkably enhanced owing to the formation of sandwichlike ternary heterojunctions. This work provides a new direction for the development of MOF-based hybrids with controllable compositions, structures, and functionalities.
